<package xmlns="http://www.idpf.org/2007/opf" unique-identifier="p9780141902876" version="2.0">
<metadata xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">
			<dc:title>Rashomon and Seventeen Other Stories</dc:title>
			<dc:creator>Ryunosuke Akutagawa</dc:creator>
			<dc:date>2006</dc:date>
			<dc:identifier id="p9780141902876">9780141902876</dc:identifier>
                        <dc:format>320 pages</dc:format>
			<dc:coverage>London</dc:coverage>
			<dc:coverage>New York</dc:coverage>
			<dc:coverage>Camberwell</dc:coverage>
			<dc:coverage>Ontario</dc:coverage>
			<dc:coverage>New Delhi</dc:coverage>
			<dc:coverage>Auckland</dc:coverage>
			<dc:coverage>Rosebank</dc:coverage>
			<dc:type>Text</dc:type>
			<dc:language>en</dc:language>
			<dc:rights>All rights reserved.</dc:rights>
			<dc:publisher>Penguin Books Ltd</dc:publisher>
	<description xmlns="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">&lt;p&gt;Ryünosuke Akutagawa (1892-1927) is one of Japan's foremost stylists - a modernist master whose short stories are marked by highly original imagery, cynicism, beauty and wild humour. 'Rashömon' and 'In a Bamboo Grove' inspired Kurosawa's magnificent film and depict a past in which morality is turned upside down, while tales such as 'The Nose', 'O-Gin' and 'Loyalty' paint a rich and imaginative picture of a medieval Japan peopled by Shoguns and priests, vagrants and peasants. And in later works such as 'Death Register', 'The Life of a Stupid Man' and 'Spinning Gears', Akutagawa drew from his own life to devastating effect, revealing his intense melancholy and terror of madness in exquisitely moving impressionistic stories.</description>
</metadata>
